I wrote the 2400-baud version with raw text and no compression. I tested the encoder and decoder through simulated audio (WAV and in-memory), not through a real sound card, so the live --play and --live modes are untested. The test results are below.

What it does

Modulation: 2400 baud at 48 kHz, which is exactly 20 samples per bit. A 1 is 7200 Hz and a 0 is 4800 Hz.
Frames: each frame carries 200 text bytes, with a header, a CRC16, and 16 Reed-Solomon parity bytes. Reed-Solomon can repair up to 8 corrupted bytes per frame. I wrote it myself, so you don't need to install anything except numpy.
Per-frame resync: every frame has its own preamble and sync word, so clock drift between the two sound cards can't build up over a long message.
Receiver: it finds every frame in a recording, reassembles them in order, and tells you which frames are missing, if any.
Repeat option: --repeat N sends the whole message N times. Since the cable is one-way, this is your fallback if a frame is lost.

Test results (simulated audio)

15,000 characters took 58.2 s, which works out to about 15,400 characters per minute.
Decoding was perfect with heavy added noise.
Decoding was perfect with a simulated 100 ppm clock mismatch between sender and receiver.
Decoding was perfect with a 25 ms dropout in the middle of the audio.

Using it

# PC1 (sender)
python audiomodem2400.py send @story.txt --play --repeat 2

# PC2 (receiver) - start this first, give it enough seconds
python audiomodem2400.py recv --live 90

Use --wav instead of --play or --live if you want to record to or read from a file. Both sound cards should be set to 48 kHz. The --live and --play options need pip install sounddevice.

Before the first real run

Start with --amp 0.3 and a low PC1 output volume. Raise the volume until PC2's recording level peaks around 50-70%, without clipping.
Turn off AGC, noise suppression and any "enhancements" on PC2's microphone input. If the PC has a line-in, use that instead.
Start the receiver before the sender, and give --live a few seconds more than the expected audio length, since 58 s is the figure for 15,000 characters.